This readme is mostly notes that I think are important, so please read all
of it before building the project.  Some of it doesn't follow any real
pattern, but it is a good idea to read this before you do much with this
package.

First off, I would like to thank you for your interest in this project.
It is gratifying to see people download it.  I would ask that you send me
an e-mail through my sourceforge account letting me know who you are, what
you are intending to use this for, and any questions you have.  I know
the documentation for this project so far blows chunks, but I'm trying to
fix that at least a little.  In the docs directory you will find -very- old
documentation regarding the version I wrote for DOS 20 years ago.  You will
also find a somewhat updated version of the documentation for the 3.2.0
release.  It doesn't include the API for C++, Java or PHP.  That should be
forthcoming....

There is now a website that is getting some more updated web-based
documentation.  The address is http://www.super-user.biz/dataman.  So far
there is general documentation, and the C and PHP function references.  Be
sure to look at that, as it is, at present, the most current information.

The Dataman database system was completely re-written beginning with version
3.  This version replaced the old system where the API library directly
modified the database.  In it the system was redesigned as a database server
with client API libraries.

The server was designed to be fully multi-threaded, have shared and mutually
exclusive locks, and tries to maintain concurrency.  The 'C' API library
maintained full backward computability with previous versions; application
code required only a re-compile to function in the new system.  Also added
were API libraries for C++ and eventually Java, and a PHP module.  The reason
for this project was 1) to bring those original installations using the prior
system out of the stone age, and 2) to be a very small and fast server,
adequate for running on small hosts.

The system is typically distributed as a source.tar.gz package.  To build and
install it, the system owner should put the source package in a temporary
directory, then execute the command:

tar xzvf dataman-<version>.tar.gz   (on Linux systems)
or
zcat dataman-<version>.tar.gz | tar xvf   (for other unixes)

this will create the sub directory dataman.<version>, with all of the
appropriate source contained in it.  Change to that directory, then issue
the commands

./configure
make
make install (or) make install-strip

This will build the server and all of the client libraries.  The
'make install-strip' command installs with debugging symbols stripped from
the binaries.

The text mode windowing system is set to initialize by default in the C and
C++ libraries.  If you don't want it to, modify the config.h.in file before
you run configure to comment out the line at the top that defines DWINDOW.
You can still use the windowing routines, but you will have to manually
initialize and terminate the routines.

Currently, the system is hard coded to build the Java classlibs, so if you
don't have the Sun Java development kit installed and in your path (javac),
the build will fail.  If you don't want the Java classlibs built, then you
can tweak the Makefile.am file.  Change the line

SUBDIRS=c++ c java

to read

SUBDIRS=c++ c

then from the base directory run automake (or autoreconf), configure, make,
make install.

After you have run the 'make install' command, the system will be installed
in the place your configure command specified.  The default location is
/usr/local.  There will be dataman commands installed in <default_dir>/bin,
libraries in <default_dir>/lib, include files in
<default_dir>/include/dataman, and the Java classlibs will be in
<default_dir>/java/Dataman.  It might be advisable to add the following lines
to your /etc/services files

dataman	8758/tcp	# dataman database server
dataman	8758/udp	# dataman database server

Oh, and another thing... there should be either a dataman user or an alias
for your mailer, because when the main executive catches an abnormal term
of either of the servers it sends a notification to the 'dataman' mail
account.

To start the server execute the command (either from the command line, or an
rc script)

dataman

This will start the three processes that control the dataman services.  The
executive process - dataman monitors the other two, dataman_con, and
dataman_srv.  The first is the dataman connection server, and the other is
the database server.  As new connections are accepted from clients, there
will be a new dataman_con process for each active connection.  If you are
running Linux kernel version 2.4 or earlier, you will also see a dataman_srv
entry for each active server thread.

The usage for the server process is:

dataman: usage: dataman [-D|n num|q|s|t|v]
    -D  start with debugging turned on
    -m size where size is the size of the shared memory
            segment
    -n num where num is the numer of worker threads
           in the database server
    -q  query status of dataman
    -s  controlled shutdown of dataman processes
    -t  forced termination of dataman processes
    -v  print version number and exit

Starting the dataman process with debugging on produces copious amounts of
data in the /tmp directory.

The -m switch is new in version 3.3.0.  Each connection has it's own shared
memory segment that is used to communicate with the database server.  This
lets you specify the size in Kilobytes of the segment.  The size is, at
present, capped at 4K.  You may specify an integer value of 1, 2, 3, or 4.

The -n num switch is new with version 3.3.0.  Originally, a new thread was
started for each request and terminated at the end of the request.  Now a
number of worker threads are started at system startup.  With this argument
you may specify the number of threads in the pool.  If not specified, the
number of threads defaults to 10.

To shut down the dataman process, issue the dataman command with either the
-s or -t switch.  The -s switch will signal the dataman process to accept no
new connections, and when the last connection is terminated by the client, to
shut down all of the remaining processes.  The -t switch will terminate 'with
prejudice' all of the server processes.

The system is not an SQL database, though an SQL layer could easily be built
on top of it.  The closest description for it is as an ISAM style database.
Data relationships can be flat, or hierarchical, but of course the latter
requires a good database design.  Records can 'belong' to other records.
This is by virtue of data record placement.  Records may be grouped together
by virtue of how they are inserted into the database.  Unlike SQL databases,
the structure and order of the data records are determinate.

The databases consist of records or 'rows' in a file.  A record consists of
a set of fields or 'columns'.  There may be many different types of records
in a particular file, and one or many files may be used for a database.  This
is how data ownership is achieved.  If, for example, a database contained
information regarding families, there would be a general record that refers
to the entire family, and then because you can determine the order of the
data records, all of the records describing that particular family's members
would immediately follow.

In an index a key will refer to a particular record in a data file.  An index
can have references to many different data files.  Thus, with appropriate use
of multi format data files, and having more than one index open at a time,
the user can have access to very large amounts of data.

